# Line endings are part of a content address here, so they cannot be left to
# the platform.
#
# export/registry.py pins `reportSha256` over the BYTES of a parity report,
# and `verify` re-hashes the file in the working tree. With no policy, a
# checkout on a machine with core.autocrlf=true renders an LF blob as CRLF,
# so the same commit produces different bytes on different machines. That
# broke both ways: two engine records were registered on Windows and pinned
# the CRLF rendering (fixed in 08cd747), and `verify` on that same checkout
# still disagreed with an LF pin afterwards — the write side and the read
# side were each hashing a local rendering rather than the file.
#
# Forcing eol=lf makes the working tree match the blob on every platform, so
# a hash taken anywhere is the hash of the same content. Every committed blob
# is already LF, so this changes nothing that exists: no blob is rewritten
# and no pinned hash is invalidated. It constrains future checkouts and
# future registrations.
#
# CONVERTING AN EXISTING CRLF WORKING TREE — and `git add --renormalize .`
# is NOT the command, despite being the usual advice. Measured on a Windows
# checkout: --renormalize re-stages blobs under the current attributes, and
# every blob here is already LF, so it is a no-op. `git checkout-index -f -a`
# is a no-op too: under core.autocrlf a CRLF working file is considered
# unchanged against its LF blob, so git sees nothing to do. Both commands
# report success and convert nothing, which sends you round the loop —
# run it, see no change, run verify, still get CHANGED.
#
# What works is deleting the files so git has to write them again:
#
#     git ls-files -z | xargs -0 rm -f && git checkout -- .
#
# A fresh clone gets it right with no intervention.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

# Model artifacts and archives are bytes, never text. Normalising one would
# corrupt it, and these are the files whose hashes matter most.
*.onnx    binary
*.plan    binary
*.engine  binary
*.pt      binary
*.pth     binary
*.gz      binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
